WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.819
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann.

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:06.540
Um, today I want to talk about
language learning apps, language

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.260
learning tools, the language
learning environment that we live in.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.950
Uh, remember if you enjoy
these videos, please subscribe.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.850
You know, you can click on
the bell to get notifications.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.380
Um, I've been learning
languages since, I don't know...

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.530
I mean, we had French at school
and I got particularly keen

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:28.620
as a 17 year old, 18 year old.

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:34.019
In Montreal at the McGill university,
uh, ultimately went to France, uh,

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.339
studied Chinese, studied Japanese, or
learned it, living in Japan and had

00:00:38.339 --> 00:00:39.720
learned a bunch of other languages.

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:48.209
And that goes back over 50 years, never
has it been easier to learn languages.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.030
And part of the reason is because
of the variety of call them

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.330
language apps, uh, language tools.

00:00:57.825 --> 00:01:02.085
Uh, language services that
are available to us today.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:04.965
And I'm only going to touch on a
few of them because I can't remember

00:01:04.965 --> 00:01:10.515
them all, but let's just start
with, you know, uh, smartphones.

00:01:11.715 --> 00:01:20.925
I carry in my iPhone, a language lab,
a range of audio and text material that

00:01:20.925 --> 00:01:24.225
will be the envy of any language lab

00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:32.405
50 20, 30, 40 years ago, the range of
material that I have, and if my phone

00:01:32.405 --> 00:01:38.255
gets full, I can park them into my
computer and get them later so that,

00:01:38.285 --> 00:01:41.345
that, and I can listen on my iPhone.

00:01:41.645 --> 00:01:44.015
I can do LingQ on my iPhone.

00:01:44.045 --> 00:01:45.875
I can read, I can look things up.

00:01:46.325 --> 00:01:53.550
I can go to Google Translate on my
iPhone or other dictionaries and I'm

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.850
just touching the surface because
other people, I mean, the range of

00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.830
language apps that you can find in
the, in the App Store is, is enormous.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.620
It's so many that you, you don't know
which ones are going to be useful.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:10.410
Many of them are not very useful, but
I think the, the smartphone itself,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.910
and of course, you know, similar to
the smartphone, you have the iPad,

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.670
which I tend to use more for language
learning, especially if I'm working

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.730
on LingQ or, uh, the iPod touch,
which I use when I go jogging or when

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.100
I, you know, don't want to take my
phone with me cause it's too heavy.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:28.970
Basically

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.110
providing the same range of services,
including, you know, studying on LingQ.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.210
So that's to start with.

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:41.505
Uh, MP3 technology of course
has made it so much easier to

00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:43.334
access audio than ever before.

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:45.315
You don't have to go buy CDs.

00:02:45.315 --> 00:02:48.255
You don't have open reel tapes.

00:02:48.554 --> 00:02:51.584
You don't have, um, you
know, cassette tapes.

00:02:51.885 --> 00:02:55.005
You just have MP3 files, which you
send back and forth to your friends,

00:02:55.185 --> 00:02:57.315
which you can find you can download.

00:02:57.315 --> 00:02:58.845
You can subscribe to podcasts.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.380
There's another call it service.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:06.480
The range of language podcasts, not
only aimed at learners, but what I

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.430
prefer to use is podcasts aimed at the
native speaker on a variety of subjects.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:19.950
Um, you know, YouTube, YouTube
has a great variety of, uh,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.080
videos, some aimed at the learner,

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:22.980
some not.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.520
Very often, subtitles are
provided in the target language.

00:03:27.135 --> 00:03:31.095
Uh, with that, I can import those
into LingQ and study them as lessons.

00:03:31.515 --> 00:03:33.704
So there's an unlimited
supply in certain languages,

00:03:33.734 --> 00:03:36.734
unfortunately, not in all languages.

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:39.975
And there are issues, for example,
learning in Standard Arabic.

00:03:40.015 --> 00:03:44.745
There are very few videos
with both the audio and the

00:03:44.745 --> 00:03:46.095
subtitles in standard Arabic.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.975
So there's an issue there, but
nevertheless, there's so much

00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:50.894
there that you can go to now.

00:03:51.525 --> 00:03:54.855
In order to make sense of what
I'm listening to, uh, whether it

00:03:54.855 --> 00:03:58.215
be podcasts or, or even Netflix,
I shouldn't leave out Netflix.

00:03:58.215 --> 00:04:00.255
We can download series there.

00:04:00.255 --> 00:04:02.445
We can download the dialogue to LingQ.

00:04:02.505 --> 00:04:05.985
We can't capture the audio, but
I can go through the dialogue

00:04:05.985 --> 00:04:07.395
and learn the words and phrases.

00:04:07.575 --> 00:04:10.425
And of course their online
dictionaries become important.

00:04:10.755 --> 00:04:14.235
Online dictionaries are extremely
important to LingQ because even

00:04:14.265 --> 00:04:17.205
in every language, the learner,
depending on their native language

00:04:17.235 --> 00:04:18.945
can look up with the lang...

00:04:19.005 --> 00:04:20.565
with the dictionary of their choice.

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:24.015
There are certain choice dictionaries
that are particularly useful.

00:04:24.555 --> 00:04:29.325
I am told that Jisho in
Japanese is particularly good.

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:33.975
I don't study Japanese, so I have no way
of commenting other than apparently it's

00:04:33.975 --> 00:04:37.815
an excellent dictionary, which provides
you with stroke order for characters,

00:04:37.995 --> 00:04:42.315
different meanings, different phrases, uh,
that use the words that you're looking up.

00:04:42.735 --> 00:04:46.605
Uh, I find Context
Reverso to be tremendous.

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:50.854
Gives you a range and this is typical of
what the online dictionaries provide now

00:04:50.854 --> 00:04:53.315
is a range of phrases using the word.

00:04:54.604 --> 00:04:56.375
Very often they'll give
you text to speech.

00:04:57.695 --> 00:05:02.135
Context Reverso has a conjugating
dictionary and there are other

00:05:02.224 --> 00:05:05.674
conjugating dictionaries so that if you
look up a word, you can immediately see

00:05:06.155 --> 00:05:07.785
the conjugation or...

00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:09.645
it's not so common for declensions.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:13.785
Unfortunately, the declensions being,
you know, nouns and adjectives, which

00:05:13.785 --> 00:05:19.665
change form, but for verbs it's quite
common to have the ability to go and see

00:05:19.665 --> 00:05:25.845
sort of the conjugation table, uh, which
I never tried to remember, but as I look

00:05:25.845 --> 00:05:31.605
at it more often, I get closer and closer
to a sense of how, how that verb works.

00:05:31.995 --> 00:05:35.565
Remembering always that it's going to
be a lot of exposure before it starts to

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.909
click in, but I mentioned text to speech.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:40.979
There's a tremendous
resource, text to speech.

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:45.180
If I'm reading something in particular,
in Arabic, where it's hard to tell just

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.240
how something is pronounced text to
speech tells me how it's pronounced.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.719
Now, I can't listen to a whole lesson
in text to speech, but I can listen to

00:05:51.719 --> 00:05:53.700
words and phrases in text to speech.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.060
I still don't have text to speech in
Persian, which is very unfortunate,

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:02.640
but we haven't been able to find a text
to speech service that fits with the

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.840
other service that we provide at LingQ.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:10.050
Uh, Google Translate what a
tremendous service, you know, I, I,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.435
uh, have my, uh, Uh, you know, my
online sessions with tutors and I

00:06:15.465 --> 00:06:18.675
struggle to say certain things and
even after I'm still struggling.

00:06:18.675 --> 00:06:19.784
How do you actually say that?

00:06:19.784 --> 00:06:24.164
It, because the phrasing is kind of
mysterious and, and so I'll go to Google

00:06:24.164 --> 00:06:26.505
Translate and I type it in, in English.

00:06:26.715 --> 00:06:27.795
I want to go.

00:06:27.795 --> 00:06:28.695
I went yesterday.

00:06:28.695 --> 00:06:29.355
I should go.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:35.505
Anything that, you know, has a verb form
or tense, or mood, or some structure

00:06:35.505 --> 00:06:40.740
that I find difficult to get a hang
of in the language that I'm learning.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:42.240
I just type it in, in English.

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.460
I get it in the target language.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:51.170
And so I, I often go to Google Translate,
not only to look up the meaning of a word

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:55.280
in the foreign language that I'm learning,
but also to see how I would go about

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.370
saying something in that target language.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.290
So that's a tremendous app.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.260
Um, I mentioned podcasts, many podcasts,
perhaps most don't have transcripts.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:11.070
Without the transcript I'm kind of
in trouble because, uh, depending

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.280
on how far along I am in the
language, I may only understand

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.350
20 or 30% without the transcript.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.530
With the transcript I can import
it into LingQ, look up the words,

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.830
learn the words, but without the
transcripts, it's very difficult.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.470
And it's frustrating to listen
to things over and over again

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.460
that you don't understand.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:31.770
So automatic transcription services, which
I mentioned before, like Happyscribe.

00:07:32.505 --> 00:07:35.294
Quite, I mean, remarkably
accurate and they're all

00:07:35.294 --> 00:07:36.945
getting more and more accurate.

00:07:37.155 --> 00:07:40.244
Like all of these apps are
getting better and better.

00:07:40.335 --> 00:07:44.385
I can remember even with LingQ when I
was, you know, I was taking three or four

00:07:44.385 --> 00:07:50.594
seconds to look up a word and, and now
it's, it's instant and I don't want to

00:07:50.594 --> 00:07:52.875
talk too much about LingQ 5.0, because,

00:07:53.325 --> 00:07:57.555
because Mark tells me not to talk about
it because it's so complex because

00:07:57.555 --> 00:08:02.295
we are, we are, um, writing code, not
me, but the people within our team are

00:08:02.295 --> 00:08:09.525
writing code for the iOS system for
Android and for the, the web application.

00:08:09.765 --> 00:08:14.025
And so there's the back
and that has to be changed.

00:08:14.025 --> 00:08:19.275
And there's, there's, it just it's
taking much longer than we expected,

00:08:19.485 --> 00:08:21.615
but it is going to be even better.

00:08:21.945 --> 00:08:26.685
So, um, just as you know, Google
Translate was much worse before.

00:08:26.745 --> 00:08:30.795
Right now, Google Translate, which
is also sort of machine translation

00:08:30.825 --> 00:08:33.225
AI is remarkably accurate.

00:08:33.615 --> 00:08:37.725
Uh, automatic transcription is
remarkably accurate and all of these

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.855
things are only going to get better.

00:08:40.935 --> 00:08:44.895
Similarly with LingQ, I think it's
only going to get better, again, an

00:08:44.895 --> 00:08:46.515
indication of the world we live in.

00:08:46.875 --> 00:08:50.865
We have developers working on the
LingQ project with my son, Mark.

00:08:50.865 --> 00:08:57.660
I'm not involved, uh, from, uh,
okay ukraine, korea, Portugal,

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:01.140
two in Ghana, uh, one in Bolivia.

00:09:01.439 --> 00:09:04.800
Um, we have, uh, you know, our
customer service people in Serbia.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:11.310
It's an absolute, it's an international
cooperative venture, uh, which is again,

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:15.780
an indication of the world we live in
where we are connecting and because we

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.300
can connect so easily with, with people.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.790
And I did mention, I think
I mentioned italki, there's

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.490
another wonderful application.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:29.160
They have such a wide range of
tutors, uh, with different price

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.960
levels and different skill sets.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.460
And you can choose the one you want.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:37.230
Uh, we also offer, uh, tutoring at
LingQ, but we don't have the range

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:38.770
of tutors that they have at italki.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.200
So, you know, and there again,
I've only touched on a few things.

00:09:43.485 --> 00:09:45.325
There are people who use Anki, Memrize.

00:09:45.345 --> 00:09:47.655
I don't use those, so I don't want
to talk too much about them because

00:09:47.655 --> 00:09:49.125
I'm not that familiar with them.

00:09:49.665 --> 00:09:52.275
Uh, so this is the world we live in.

00:09:52.455 --> 00:09:56.625
This is the world we live in so that
language learning today, because of

00:09:56.625 --> 00:10:02.885
this abundance of, of, uh, wonderful
functionality that people are creating...

00:10:02.965 --> 00:10:06.335
and six months from now, there
will be people who will have

00:10:06.335 --> 00:10:12.569
created other, um, you know, apps
that help us learn languages.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:16.800
So whenever I hear people say, well,
you know, with AI, we won't need to

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.250
learn languages because we'll just
be able to talk to the machine and

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.100
then the machine will translate and
the conversation will go that way.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:24.480
I don't believe it for a minute.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.770
I think the joy of communicating
face-to-face with someone in another

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:33.300
language, the joy of discovering
their culture, as I have been doing

00:10:33.329 --> 00:10:38.760
now, talking to my tutors in Iran and
discovering that one lives in ... Which

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.405
is in a, in Golestan in the North.

00:10:43.155 --> 00:10:44.925
Is that what I, I can't remember anymore.

00:10:45.195 --> 00:10:49.395
One lived in Lahijan ... I think.

00:10:49.445 --> 00:10:49.955
I can't remember.

00:10:49.955 --> 00:10:51.425
See, that's the other thing
you forget everything.

00:10:51.695 --> 00:10:53.465
But if I got into the
conversation, I'd remember.

00:10:53.555 --> 00:10:56.255
But so you're connecting,
you're learning languages.

00:10:56.255 --> 00:10:57.365
You're connecting with people.

00:10:57.365 --> 00:10:59.105
Why would people give that up?

00:10:59.285 --> 00:11:00.905
Just in order to talk to a machine?

00:11:01.055 --> 00:11:01.685
I don't see it.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.570
The language apps are making the
language journey easier, and people are

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.460
going to be more and more successful.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:12.300
And so I see a very bright
future for language learning.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:13.470
So thank you for listening.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:14.430
Bye for now.

